# fastlane/Fastfile — TestFlight/App Store automation for ZANO.
#
# See docs/spec.md §12 (Tech Stack table's CI row: "Xcode Cloud or GitHub Actions + fastlane" —
# "TestFlight in one command") and docs/setup/apple-developer.md ("Also needed before TestFlight") for the
# product/process context this implements. Invoked by .github/workflows/ci.yml's `testflight` job
# (currently still gated off — see that file) via `fastlane beta`, and by hand from a Mac once one
# exists (`bundle exec fastlane beta` from the repo root).
#
# ── Status: unverified ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Written on Windows with no Mac, no Xcode, and no fastlane install to run this against (see
# CLAUDE.md "Current environment status"). Every action name, parameter, and lane below is written
# from training knowledge of fastlane's documented API surface, not confirmed against a real run.
# Treat this as a strong first draft to smoke-test end-to-end the first time a Mac + real secrets
# exist, not as proven-working automation. Specific known-uncertain points are flagged inline and
# summarized at the bottom of this file.
#
# ── Required secrets (GitHub Actions repo secrets, or exported locally) ────────────────────────────
# None of these exist yet — see docs/setup/apple-developer.md and docs/PROGRESS.md. Naming below
# matches what .github/workflows/ci.yml's commented-out testflight job already names
# (APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY, MATCH_GIT_URL, MATCH_PASSWORD) plus the few more that same job's
# "etc." implied would be needed once actually wired up:
#
#   APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY   Base64-encoded JSON blob: {"key_id": "...", "issuer_id": "...",
#                                "key": "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----"}
#                                Assemble this by hand after generating an App Store Connect API key
#                                (App Store Connect → Users and Access → Integrations → Keys):
#                                Apple only lets you download the raw .p8 once, so capture its
#                                contents into the "key" field (escaped as \n line breaks, which is
#                                just how JSON encodes a real newline) alongside the Key ID and Issuer
#                                ID shown next to it, then `base64` the whole JSON document into this
#                                one secret. See `app_store_connect_key` lane below for the decode
#                                side.
#   APPLE_TEAM_ID                Developer Portal Team ID (10 chars, e.g. "ABCDE12345"). Shown in
#                                Apple Developer → Membership once enrolled.
#   MATCH_GIT_URL                Git URL of the private repo `fastlane match` stores signing
#                                certs/profiles in (recommended in docs/setup/apple-developer.md).
#                                Create that repo separately — this Fastfile does not create it.
#   MATCH_PASSWORD               Passphrase match uses to encrypt/decrypt that repo's contents. Read
#                                automatically by the `match` action via this exact env var name —
#                                fastlane's match config item maps 1:1 to `MATCH_PASSWORD`, so it is
#                                intentionally never passed as an explicit lane parameter below.
#
# Local (non-CI) use additionally benefits from APPLE_ID / APPLE_ITC_TEAM_ID / ZANO_APP_IDENTIFIER —
# see fastlane/Appfile.
#
# ── What's deliberately NOT here yet ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# - fastlane/metadata, fastlane/screenshots: no App Store listing copy, screenshots, or privacy
#   nutrition-label answers exist yet (see docs/setup/app-review-notes.md checklist). `release`
#   below skips both (`skip_metadata`/`skip_screenshots`) until those exist — don't flip that
#   without also adding the directories `deliver` expects.
# - Apple Watch (ZANOWatch): excluded from signing/build entirely. project.yml's own header notes
#   ZANOWatch isn't embedded as ZANO's companion yet, and the v1 target is iPhone-only
#   (`TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY: "1"` in project.yml). Add it here only once that's true.

default_platform(:ios)

# ── Shared constants ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SCHEME       = "ZANO"
PROJECT_FILE = "ZANO.xcodeproj"
OUTPUT_DIR   = "build"

# Bundle identifiers as XcodeGen actually derives them TODAY from project.yml's
# `options.bundleIdPrefix: com.zano.app` with no `PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER` override on any of
# these targets — XcodeGen's default is "<bundleIdPrefix>.<target name>" when a target doesn't
# override it. (project.yml's own comment block above its `ZANOWatch` target spells out this exact
# derivation rule, for the same reason: ZANOWatch *does* override it, and explains why.)
#
# ⚠️ KNOWN, FLAGGED MISMATCH — not fixed here, out of this task's file list (fastlane/*, ci.yml
# only): docs/setup/apple-developer.md describes registering "com.zano.app" (bare, no suffix) as
# the *main app's* bundle ID, with "5 extension suffixes" hung off it. As project.yml is actually
# written today, the main `ZANO` target would generate "com.zano.app.ZANO", not bare
# "com.zano.app". Someone needs to either add an explicit `PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER: com.zano.app`
# to the `ZANO` target in project.yml (the same fix already applied to `ZANOWatch` right below it,
# for the identical reason) or update the docs to match reality. Whichever way that lands, these
# constants — and the App Store Connect app record / match's stored profiles — must be updated to
# match whatever XcodeGen *actually* generates. Verify with
# `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings -project ZANO.xcodeproj -target ZANO | grep PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER`
# on a Mac before trusting these for a real submission.
TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS = {
  "ZANO"            => "com.zano.app.ZANO",
  "ZANOWidgets"     => "com.zano.app.ZANOWidgets",
  "ZANOShieldConfig" => "com.zano.app.ZANOShieldConfig",
  "ZANOShieldAction" => "com.zano.app.ZANOShieldAction",
  "ZANOMonitor"     => "com.zano.app.ZANOMonitor",
  "ZANOReport"      => "com.zano.app.ZANOReport"
}.freeze

BUNDLE_ID_APP = TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS.fetch("ZANO")

platform :ios do
  # ── Private helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  desc "Raise a clear error (not a stack trace) listing exactly which required secrets are missing"
  private_lane :ensure_secrets_present do |options|
    names = options.fetch(:names)
    missing = names.select { |n| ENV[n].to_s.strip.empty? }
    next if missing.empty?

    UI.user_error!(
      "Missing required secret(s): #{missing.join(', ')}. " \
      "These come from GitHub Actions repo secrets in CI, or your local shell env otherwise — " \
      "see the header comment in fastlane/Fastfile and docs/setup/apple-developer.md for what " \
      "each one is and how to create it. None of them exist yet as of docs/PROGRESS.md's last " \
      "update, so seeing this locally or in a real CI run is expected until Apple Developer " \
      "Program enrollment + signing are set up."
    )
  end

  desc "Regenerate ZANO.xcodeproj from project.yml via XcodeGen (never hand-edit the generated project)"
  private_lane :generate_xcode_project do
    # CI's testflight job installs xcodegen via `brew install xcodegen fastlane` before calling any
    # lane here (see .github/workflows/ci.yml); a local Mac run needs it on PATH already
    # (docs/setup/mac-setup.md). Run from the repo root, one level up from fastlane/.
    Dir.chdir("..") do
      sh("xcodegen generate")
    end
  end

  desc "Build (or reuse, if already loaded) the App Store Connect API key from APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY"
  private_lane :app_store_connect_key do
    ensure_secrets_present(names: ["APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY"])

    require "base64"
    require "json"

    raw = ENV.fetch("APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY")
    parsed = begin
      JSON.parse(Base64.decode64(raw))
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      UI.user_error!(
        "APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY did not decode to valid JSON after base64 decoding " \
        "(#{e.message}). Expected shape: " \
        '{"key_id": "...", "issuer_id": "...", "key": "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----"}' \
        " — see the header comment in this Fastfile for how to assemble it."
      )
    end

    %w[key_id issuer_id key].each do |field|
      UI.user_error!("APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY JSON is missing required field \"#{field}\"") unless parsed[field]
    end

    app_store_connect_api_key(
      key_id: parsed.fetch("key_id"),
      issuer_id: parsed.fetch("issuer_id"),
      key_content: parsed.fetch("key"),
      is_key_content_base64: false,
      in_house: false
    )
  end

  desc "Fetch this app's signing certs/profiles from the match repo (manual signing, App Store distribution)"
  private_lane :setup_appstore_signing do
    ensure_secrets_present(names: ["MATCH_GIT_URL", "MATCH_PASSWORD", "APPLE_TEAM_ID"])

    # MATCH_PASSWORD is intentionally not passed explicitly — fastlane's `match` action reads it
    # from that exact env var name automatically (documented match ↔ env var convention), the same
    # way `password:` would resolve if this ran interactively and prompted for it.
    #
    # `type: "appstore"` (no hyphen) is match's own naming for this signing type — distinct from
    # `build_app`'s `export_method: "app-store"` (hyphenated) used below. Confusing but correct as
    # of the fastlane versions this was written against from training knowledge; flagged in this
    # file's bottom-of-file "known-uncertain" list in case a newer fastlane release has changed it.
    match(
      type: "appstore",
      git_url: ENV.fetch("MATCH_GIT_URL"),
      app_identifier: TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS.values,
      # GitHub Actions sets CI=true in every job's env automatically; readonly there so a CI run
      # only ever *fetches* certs/profiles match already has, never generates new ones. A developer
      # running this by hand locally (CI unset) gets normal read/write match behavior.
      readonly: ENV["CI"] == "true",
      team_id: ENV.fetch("APPLE_TEAM_ID")
    )

    profile_mapping = lane_context[SharedValues::MATCH_PROVISIONING_PROFILE_MAPPING]

    # Point every target at its own match-managed profile individually — `update_code_signing_settings`
    # applies one (identity, profile) pair per call, and each ZANO extension target needs its own
    # distinct profile/bundle ID, not the main app's.
    TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS.each do |target_name, bundle_id|
      update_code_signing_settings(
        use_automatic_signing: false,
        path: PROJECT_FILE,
        targets: [target_name],
        team_id: ENV.fetch("APPLE_TEAM_ID"),
        code_sign_identity: "Apple Distribution",
        bundle_identifier: bundle_id,
        profile_name: profile_mapping[bundle_id]
      )
    end

    profile_mapping
  end

  desc "Archive + export a release-configuration .ipa signed for App Store distribution"
  private_lane :build_appstore_ipa do |options|
    profile_mapping = options.fetch(:profile_mapping)

    build_app(
      scheme: SCHEME,
      project: PROJECT_FILE,
      configuration: "Release",
      export_method: "app-store",
      output_directory: OUTPUT_DIR,
      output_name: "ZANO.ipa",
      clean: true,
      export_options: {
        method: "app-store",
        signingStyle: "manual",
        teamID: ENV.fetch("APPLE_TEAM_ID"),
        provisioningProfiles: profile_mapping
      }
    )
  end

  # ── Public lanes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  desc "Local convenience: regenerate the Xcode project from project.yml (`fastlane generate`)"
  lane :generate do
    generate_xcode_project
  end

  desc "Local convenience: run Core's unit tests the same way CI's build-and-test job does"
  lane :tests do
    Dir.chdir("..") do
      sh("swift test --package-path Core")
    end
  end

  desc "Build ZANO and push a new build straight to TestFlight — called by CI's `testflight` job"
  lane :beta do
    ensure_secrets_present(names: %w[APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY APPLE_TEAM_ID MATCH_GIT_URL MATCH_PASSWORD])

    generate_xcode_project
    api_key = app_store_connect_key
    profile_mapping = setup_appstore_signing

    # Keep every TestFlight build number unique without hand-bumping anything git-tracked:
    # project.yml (the source of truth) sets no explicit build number, so this bumps it only on the
    # freshly-generated, gitignored ZANO.xcodeproj for this run alone.
    latest = latest_testflight_build_number(
      api_key: api_key,
      app_identifier: BUNDLE_ID_APP,
      initial_build_number: 0
    )
    increment_build_number(build_number: latest + 1, xcodeproj: PROJECT_FILE)

    build_appstore_ipa(profile_mapping: profile_mapping)

    upload_to_testflight(
      api_key: api_key,
      app_identifier: BUNDLE_ID_APP,
      ipa: "#{OUTPUT_DIR}/ZANO.ipa",
      skip_waiting_for_build_processing: true,
      distribute_external: false,
      changelog: ENV["ZANO_CHANGELOG"] || "Automated TestFlight build from CI."
    )
  end

  desc "App Store submission skeleton — NOT wired into CI yet, real gaps flagged inline; " \
       "see this lane's body before ever running it for a real submission"
  lane :release do
    # Everything through build/signing/upload mirrors `beta` — the parts that differ (metadata,
    # screenshots, review notes, submission itself) are exactly the parts that don't exist yet, so
    # this stays a deliberately conservative skeleton: it uploads a build and stops short of
    # actually submitting it for review.
    ensure_secrets_present(names: %w[APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY APPLE_TEAM_ID MATCH_GIT_URL MATCH_PASSWORD])

    generate_xcode_project
    api_key = app_store_connect_key
    profile_mapping = setup_appstore_signing
    build_appstore_ipa(profile_mapping: profile_mapping)

    upload_to_app_store(
      api_key: api_key,
      app_identifier: BUNDLE_ID_APP,
      ipa: "#{OUTPUT_DIR}/ZANO.ipa",
      # Do NOT flip these to false/true until the corresponding real content exists:
      skip_screenshots: true, # no fastlane/screenshots yet — nothing to upload
      skip_metadata: true, # no fastlane/metadata yet — App Store Connect listing copy isn't written
      submit_for_review: false, # flip only once docs/setup/app-review-notes.md's full checklist is
                                 # complete (demo account seeded, review notes pasted in, privacy
                                 # nutrition label filled out, Family Controls entitlement approved)
      automatic_release: false,
      force: true, # skip HTML preview/confirmation prompt — required for unattended CI use
      precheck_include_in_app_purchases: false
    )
  end
end

# ── Known-uncertain points (flagged per CLAUDE.md working rule 5 — no Mac/fastlane install to
#    check any of this against a real run) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS above assumes XcodeGen's default "<prefix>.<target name>" bundle-id
#    derivation for every target except where project.yml explicitly overrides it (only
#    ZANOWatch does, and that target is deliberately excluded here). Confirm against
#    `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings` the first time this runs on a Mac.
# 2. `sh("xcodegen generate")` / `sh("swift test --package-path Core")` use the classic
#    single-string `sh` form deliberately (not the newer splat-args form) since that's the form
#    documented consistently across fastlane versions; `Dir.chdir("..")` wrapping each is used
#    instead of relying on any particular fastlane version's default working directory for `sh`.
# 3. `match`'s `type: "appstore"` and `app_store_connect_api_key`'s exact parameter names
#    (`key_id`/`issuer_id`/`key_content`/`is_key_content_base64`/`in_house`) are written from
#    training knowledge of fastlane's documented action API; a `bundle exec fastlane action
#    app_store_connect_api_key` / `match` on a real machine is the way to confirm before the
#    first real run, since fastlane's action surface does shift across releases.
# 4. `update_code_signing_settings` looped per-target with `bundle_identifier:`/`profile_name:`
#    is the documented pattern for multi-target apps signed via match, but this repo has never
#    actually run it — six targets (main app + 5 extensions) each need their own correctly
#    provisioned entry in `profile_mapping`, which itself depends on match's app_identifier list
#    exactly matching what's registered in the Developer Portal (see mismatch flagged above
#    TARGET_BUNDLE_IDS).
# 5. `build_app`'s `export_options[:provisioningProfiles]` expects bundle-id → profile-*name*
#    (not UUID); `MATCH_PROVISIONING_PROFILE_MAPPING` is documented to hold exactly that shape,
#    but hasn't been observed against a real match run in this repo.
# 6. aps-environment in project.yml's ZANO target is currently hardcoded to "development" (see
#    project.yml). A real App Store archive typically needs "production" for push to work in
#    production — that's project.yml's concern, not this file's (out of this task's scope), but
#    it will silently ship a build with the wrong push environment until someone fixes it there.
